\f1\fs48 \cf2 \cb3 Step 1: Create an EKS Cluster\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 Option A: Quick One-Liner\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 You can create a basic EKS cluster with default configurations in a single command:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf12 \cb3 eksctl\cf13  create cluster\cf16  \\\cf9 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf16   --name\cf13  my-eks-lab\cf16  \\\cf9 \
\cf16   --region\cf13  us-east-1\cf16  \\\cf9 \
\cf16   --nodegroup-name\cf13  standard-workers\cf16  \\\cf9 \
\cf16   --node-type\cf13  t3.medium\cf16  \\\cf9 \
\cf16   --nodes 3\cf9 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf10 \
\cb3 Copy\cb1 \
\cf14 \
\cb15 Explain\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 This command:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa120\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf10 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Creates an EKS cluster named 
\f5 my-eks-lab
\f1  in 
\f5 us-east-1
\f1 .\cb1 \
\ls2\ilvl0\cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Provisions a node group (named 
\f5 standard-workers
\f1 ) with 3 
\f5 t3.medium
\f1  EC2 instances.\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls2\ilvl0\cf10 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Sets up a VPC, subnets, security groups, and IAM roles automatically.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f6\i\b \cf10 \cb3 Note
\f4\b0 : You can replace 
\f7 us-east-1
\f4 , 
\f7 t3.medium
\f4 , or 
\f7 3
\f4  with your own region, instance types, and desired number of nodes.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\f1\i0\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 Option B: Using a Config File\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 For more advanced or reproducible setups, define a YAML configuration:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf17 \cb3 # eks-cluster-config.yaml\cf9 \
 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0
\cf18 apiVersion\cf19 : \cf13 eksctl.io/v1alpha5\cf9 \
\cf18 kind\cf19 : \cf13 ClusterConfig\cf9 \
 \
\cf18 metadata\cf19 :\cf9 \
\cf18   name\cf19 : \cf13 my-eks-lab\cf9 \
\cf18   region\cf19 : \cf13 us-east-2\cf9 \
 \
\cf18 nodeGroups\cf19 :\cf9 \
\cf19   - \cf18 name\cf19 : \cf13 ng-lab\cf9 \
\cf18     instanceType\cf19 : \cf13 t3.large\cf9 \
\cf18     desiredCapacity\cf19 : \cf16 2\cf9 \
\cf18     privateNetworking\cf19 : \cf16 true\cf9 \
\cf18     volumeSize\cf19 : \cf16 20\cf9 \
\cf18     labels\cf19 :\cf9 \
\cf18       role\cf19 : \cf13 worker\cf9 \
\cf18     tags\cf19 :\cf9 \
\cf18       nodegroup\cf19 : \cf13 lab\cf9 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf10 \
\cb3 Copy\cb1 \
\cf14 \
\cb15 Explain\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 Then run:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf12 \cb3 eksctl\cf13  create cluster\cf16  -f\cf13  eks-cluster-config.yaml\cf9 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf10 \
\cb3 Copy\cb1 \
\cf14 \
\cb15 Explain\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 This approach is highly customizable\'97you can add multiple node groups, different instance types, GPU nodes, custom labels, and more.\cb1 \

\fs48 \cf2 \cb3 Step 2: Verify Your Cluster\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f5\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 eksctl
\f1  will take several minutes to spin up the control plane and worker nodes. Once completed, you should see a success message like:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf9 \cb3 [
\f8 \uc0\u8505 
\f1 ]  waiting for the control plane to become ready\
[
\f9 \uc0\u10004 
\f1 ]  EKS cluster "my-eks-lab" in "us-east-1" is ready\cf9 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf10 \
\cb3 Copy\cb1 \
\cf14 \
\cb15 Explain\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 Now, check that the new cluster context is set in your local 
\f5 kubeconfig
\f1 :\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf12 \cb3 kubectl\cf13  config get-contexts\cf9 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf10 \
\cb3 Copy\cb1 \
\cf14 \
\cb15 Explain\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 You should see a context named 
\f5 my-eks-lab
\f1  or something similar. To confirm that the worker nodes are registered:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf12 \cb3 kubectl\cf13  get nodes\cf9 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf10 \
\cb3 Copy\cb1 \
\cf14 \
\cb15 Explain\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 You should see 2 or 3 nodes in a 
\f5 Ready
\f1  state (depending on how many you provisioned).\cb1 \

\fs48 \cf2 \cb3 Step 3: Test the Cluster with a Simple Deployment\cb1 \

\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 Let\'92s deploy a small application to ensure everything\'92s working:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0
\f3\b \cf10 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Create
\f1\b0  a Deployment:\cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf12 \cb3 kubectl\cf13  create deployment hello-eks\cf16  --image=kicbase/echo-server:1.0\cf10 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\f5\fs32 \uc0\u8232 
\f1 \uc0\u8232 
\f0\fs24 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \uc0\u8232 \cb3 Copy\cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cb15 Explain\cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\f1\fs32 \AppleTypeServices \cf10 \cb3 \uc0\u8232 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 \cb3 This spins up a single Pod that responds to requests with some basic info\'97perfect for a quick test.\cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\f3\b \cb3 Expose
\f1\b0  it via a Service of type 
\f5 LoadBalancer
\f1 :\cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf12 \cb3 kubectl\cf13  expose deployment hello-eks\cf16  \\\cf10 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls3\ilvl0\cf16 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	2	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  --type=LoadBalancer \\\cf10 \
\ls3\ilvl0\cf16 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	3	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  --port=8080 \\\cf10 \
\ls3\ilvl0\cf16 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	4	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
  --name=hello-service\cf10 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\f5\fs32 \uc0\u8232 
\f1 \uc0\u8232 
\f0\fs24 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \uc0\u8232 \cb3 Copy\cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cb15 Explain\cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\f1\fs32 \AppleTypeServices \cf10 \cb3 \uc0\u8232 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 
\f3\b \cb3 Check
\f1\b0  the Service:\cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf12 \cb3 kubectl\cf13  get svc hello-service\cf10 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\f5\fs32 \uc0\u8232 
\f1 \uc0\u8232 
\f0\fs24 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \uc0\u8232 \cb3 Copy\cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cb15 Explain\cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\f1\fs32 \AppleTypeServices \cf10 \cb3 \uc0\u8232 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \u8232 \cb3 You\'92ll see an external EXTERNAL-IP once AWS provisions an Elastic Load Balancer. This can take a minute or two.\cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\f3\b \cb3 Test
\f1\b0  by visiting the external IP in your browser, or:\cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\fs28 \cf12 \cb3 curl\cf13  http://\cf20 <\cf13 EXTERNAL-I\cf19 P\cf20 >\cf13 :8080\cf10 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\f5\fs32 \uc0\u8232 
\f1 \uc0\u8232 
\f0\fs24 \AppleTypeServices\AppleTypeServicesF65539 \uc0\u8232 \cb3 Copy\cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf14 \uc0\u8232 \cb15 Explain\cb1 \uc0\u8232 
\f1\fs32 \AppleTypeServices \cf10 \cb3 \uc0\u8232 \cb1 \uc0\u8232 \cf9 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0
\cf10 \cb3 You should get a simple response from the echo server. If so, 
\f4\i confetti time!
\f1\i0  You\'92ve just deployed your first app on your new EKS cluster.\cb1 \

\fs48 \cf2 \cb3 Step 4: Clean Up (Optional)\cb1 \

\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 If this is just a test, you can tear down the cluster to avoid charges:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf12 \cb3 eksctl\cf13  delete cluster\cf16  --name\cf13  my-eks-lab\cf16  --region\cf13  us-east-1\cf9 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf10 \
\cb3 Copy\cb1 \
\cf14 \
\cb15 Explain\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 Or, if you used a config file:\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\partightenfactor0

\fs28 \cf12 \cb3 eksctl\cf13  delete cluster\cf16  -f\cf13  eks-cluster-config.yaml\cf9 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\qc\partightenfactor0

\f2\fs24 \cf10 \
\cb3 Copy\cb1 \
\cf14 \
\cb15 Explain\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\f1\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 This command will delete the EKS control plane, associated CloudFormation stacks, node groups, and any managed resources. Make sure you don\'92t have any critical data in the cluster!\cb1 \

\fs48 \cf2 \cb3 Optional Enhancements\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 A. Configure Autoscaling\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 Install the 
\f3\b Cluster Autoscaler
\f1\b0  to automatically scale your worker nodes based on pending Pods:\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\partightenfactor0
\ls4\ilvl0\cf10 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	1	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Create an IAM policy for the autoscaler.\cf9  \cf10 Attach the policy to your node group\'92s IAM role.\cf9  \cf10 Deploy the Cluster Autoscaler manifest.\cf9 \cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 B. Integrate Persistent Storage\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 Use EBS or EFS volumes in your cluster by creating a 
\f3\b StorageClass
\f1\b0  and 
\f3\b PersistentVolumeClaim
\f1\b0 . For example, an EBS-backed 
\f5 gp2
\f1  or 
\f5 gp3
\f1  StorageClass to dynamically provision volumes for your apps.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 C. Ingress Controller\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 Install the 
\f3\b AWS Load Balancer Controller
\f1\b0  or 
\f3\b NGINX Ingress Controller
\f1\b0  to consolidate multiple services behind a single ALB, enabling path-based or host-based routing.\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa240\partightenfactor0

\fs40 \cf2 \cb3 D. IAM Roles for Service Accounts (IRSA)\cb1 \
\pard\pardeftab720\sa320\partightenfactor0

\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 Assign fine-grained AWS permissions to specific Pods by creating IAM roles and linking them to K8s service accounts. This ensures the principle of least privilege for each microservice.\cb1 \

\fs48 \cf2 \cb3 Lab Recap\cb1 \
\pard\tx220\tx720\pardeftab720\li720\fi-720\sa120\partightenfactor0
\ls5\ilvl0
\f3\b\fs32 \cf10 \cb3 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 {\listtext	\uc0\u8226 	}\expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
Step 1
\f1\b0 : Created an EKS cluster with 
\f5 eksctl
\f1  (either via quick flags or a YAML config).\cb1 \
\ls5\ilvl0
